---
import '../styles/global.css';
import Brand from '../components/Brand';
import record from '../../docs/evidence/2026-10-15-mock-http/audited/walkthrough.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const labels: Record<string, string> = {
  'response-loss': 'Supplier saves the order, confirmation is lost',
  'repeat-dispatch-after-both-process-restarts': 'Restart and send the same order again',
  'changed-payload-held': 'Change the amount under the same order ID',
  'revoked-during-429-wait': 'Withdraw approval before the next try',
  'revoked-after-admission-may-commit': 'Withdraw approval after sending starts',
};
if (record.status !== 'passed' || record.vendorReadback.verification !== 'verified')
  throw Error('The walkthrough requires an actual verified local report.');
const source = 'https://github.com/mastodonsharkgirl/field-relay/tree/codex/field-relay-mock-http';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Field Relay — interview walkthrough</title>
    <link rel="icon" href={base + 'favicon.svg'} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Compare two order sheets, then see how the demo handles a lost reply."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="tour">
      <header class="masthead">
        <Brand href={base} />
        <nav aria-label="Walkthrough navigation">
          <a href={base + 'case-study/'}>Case study</a>
          <a href={source}>Source</a>
        </nav>
      </header>
      <section class="tour-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">A QUICK TOUR / SAMPLE DATA</p>
        <h1>Walk through Field Relay in five minutes.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Compare two order sheets and review what changed. Then see what happens when a supplier
          saves an order but the reply never comes back.
        </p>
        <p>
          I built this demo with AI coding help. The story and orders are made up. You can try the
          spreadsheet tools here. Results from the separate server tests are shown below.
        </p>
        <a class="tour-action" href={base}>Compare the sheets</a>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="compare">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 / COMPARE TWO SHEETS</p>
        <h2 id="compare">What changed in the order sheet?</h2>
        <p>
          Imagine you handle orders for a supply-chain company. A supplier sends an updated sheet.
          You need to check for changed quantities, missing orders and repeated IDs before using it.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            The sample files are ready to use. Click <strong>Reload example</strong> to start over, or
            <strong>Try an example</strong> after clearing the example.
          </li>
          <li>
            Click Changed, then order 00009. Its quantity went from 5 to 9. You can see the original
            row from each file.
          </li>
          <li>
            Choose a decision, add a reason, and save your review. Missing or repeated IDs need a
            closer look. Download your report before closing the tab.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">
          Your files stay in this browser tab. Closing it clears your work. Saving a review does not
          send an order or change either source file.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="delivery">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 / WHEN A REPLY GOES MISSING</p>
        <h2 id="delivery">Did the supplier get the order?</h2>
        <p>
          You send an order and get no reply. The supplier may still have saved it. Sending it again
          could create a second order, so the demo first checks what the supplier has on file.
        </p>
        <div class="flow" aria-label="Order recovery sequence">
          <span>Approve the order</span><span>Send with the same order key</span><span
            >Reply is lost</span
          ><span>Check the saved order</span><span>Match the details</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          The server demo remembers the approved order after a restart and limits how often it tries
          again. If the details do not match, it stops for review. Withdrawing approval stops future
          sends, but a request already being sent may still reach the supplier.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>Which parts can I try here?</summary>
          <p>
            The file comparison and the <strong>Delivery simulator lab</strong> run in the browser. The
            simulator uses its own sample orders; it does not use your comparison results. The table below
            shows saved results from a separate server test. This website is not connected to a supplier.
          </p>
        </details>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="proof">
        <p class="eyebrow">03 / WHAT I TESTED</p>
        <h2 id="proof">Saved results from the server test</h2>
        <p>
          <strong
            >{record.scenarioResults.length} tests passed; {record.vendorReadback.count} saved orders
            checked.</strong
          > These tests used made-up orders and a mock supplier. I caused failures on purpose to check
          how the demo recovered.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Recorded scenario results">
          <table>
            <thead
              ><tr
                ><th scope="col">Scenario</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col"
                  >Orders checked in this test</th
                ></tr
              ></thead
            >
            <tbody
              >{
                record.scenarioResults.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <>
                      <th scope="row">{labels[row.name] ?? row.name}</th>
                      <td>{row.status}</td>
                      <td>{row.observedCount ?? 'unverified'}</td>
                    </>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }</tbody
            >
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Recorded 15 October 2026. Source {record.metadata.sourceSha.slice(0, 7)}; clean source: {
            String(!record.metadata.dirty)
          }. Some tests check the same order, so the numbers in the table should not be added
          together.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={source + '/docs/evidence/2026-10-15-mock-http'}>Read the test reports</a>. The
          reports include failed attempts. One earlier run stopped before sending an order, but did
          not save enough detail to explain why. The demo now waits for its workers to be ready and
          saves a short error code when something fails.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="explain">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 / HOW TO EXPLAIN IT</p>
        <h2 id="explain">A short explanation</h2>
        <blockquote>
          “I built Field Relay with AI coding help. It compares two order sheets and shows what
          changed, with the original rows next to each other. I also built a separate demo for a
          harder problem: what if a supplier saves an order but you never get the reply? It checks
          what was saved before deciding whether to try again. I tested it with made-up orders.”
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Be ready to show one changed order, explain why retrying can create duplicates, and say
          what still needs work. Talk about the choices you made and what you learned.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>Where would Claude help?</summary>
          <p>
            Claude could read a supplier’s message and draft an order. The app would check the
            fields, and a person would approve them before anything was sent. Claude would not
            decide whether it had permission to send or whether delivery succeeded. This is an idea
            for later; Claude is not connected to this demo.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p>
          <a href={source + '/docs/integration/INTERVIEW_GUIDE.md'}
            >Read the complete interview guide</a
          > · <a href={base}>Back to the comparison</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
